WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:07.940
My name is Maurice Patterson.

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:11.500
I'm the owner and operator of
MMP Environmental Solutions.

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:14.430
We are a
commercial-grease-trap-cleaning service

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:16.050
and waste-management company.

00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:18.920
And today we will be cleaning
a commercial grease trap

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:20.763
in a Mediterranean restaurant.

00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:24.586
The function of a grease trap

00:00:24.586 --> 00:00:28.611
is to prevent any solids,
fats, oil, and greases

00:00:28.611 --> 00:00:31.160
from dishwasher or dishwashing sinks

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:33.173
from entering your piping system.

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:36.250
So, the cleaning process typically starts

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:39.980
with me inspecting it,
making sure it's functional.

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:42.010
Typically, you want to
clean your grease trap

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:43.580
about every three months.

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:45.380
I would say that it hasn't been serviced

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:47.630
most likely close to a year.

00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:49.202
Then we unplug the grease trap

00:00:49.202 --> 00:00:51.623
and we start to remove all the components.

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:54.830
From that step, we will prepare them

00:00:54.830 --> 00:00:56.780
to be cleaned with a pressure washer.

00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:03.760
So, right here, we're doing our pump-out.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:06.640
We have the wand connected to
the high-powered vacuum pump.

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:10.297
We'll use the wand to pump
out the entire grease trap.

00:01:10.297 --> 00:01:11.580
So, as you can see,
there's a lot of grease

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:12.896
sitting on top of the grease trap,

00:01:12.896 --> 00:01:15.729
and you can get a glimpse of the solids

00:01:15.729 --> 00:01:18.390
that have floated to the top as well.

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:19.798
For Mediterranean restaurants,

00:01:19.798 --> 00:01:21.980
you're going to get a
lot of oils, cheeses,

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:23.390
you're also going to get a lot of rice.

00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:26.122
The rice is usually the main contents

00:01:26.122 --> 00:01:27.808
that sink to the bottom of the grease trap

00:01:27.808 --> 00:01:30.190
that you're going to want
to pump out regularly.

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:31.998
I would describe it as yellow grease.

00:01:31.998 --> 00:01:34.014
So yellow grease is typically grease

00:01:34.014 --> 00:01:37.830
that's used to make fries
or things like that.

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:39.550
There's actually two
different types of grease

00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:40.533
known in the industry.

00:01:40.533 --> 00:01:42.641
You have brown grease;
that's typically grease

00:01:42.641 --> 00:01:45.140
that's scraped off from pots and pans

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:47.320
that's traveled down into your dishwasher

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:49.070
and your sink, down to your grease trap.

00:01:49.070 --> 00:01:50.290
And then you also have yellow grease.

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:52.330
You can actually sell it certain places.

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:54.092
They may recycle that grease

00:01:54.092 --> 00:01:56.350
and turn it into biofuel,
things like that.

00:01:57.340 --> 00:01:58.735
It's considered liquid gold.

00:01:58.735 --> 00:02:00.943
Some companies will
actually pay them for it,

00:02:00.943 --> 00:02:03.180
so they're incentivized to make sure

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:05.003
that it's not going down the drain.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:07.580
Here, what I'm using is a putty knife.

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:10.013
I'm scraping just old food waste

00:02:10.013 --> 00:02:11.529
that has collected on there.

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:16.210
Organic waste just turns black over time.

00:02:16.210 --> 00:02:18.800
It's oxidized and turns black.

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:20.725
It's a health risk, mainly because

00:02:20.725 --> 00:02:22.841
any type of overflows
from your grease trap

00:02:22.841 --> 00:02:24.673
may come in contact with food.

00:02:28.533 --> 00:02:30.290
We use a pressure-washing
system to make sure

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:33.883
we're getting all the
residual solids cleaned out.

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:40.750
I'm using steel wool just
to do a final scrub-down,

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:43.410
make sure I'm getting into
all the nooks and crannies,

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:44.723
all the tight spaces.

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:50.310
I do this right before
we do a final spray-down

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:51.718
with the pressure washer.

00:03:01.910 --> 00:03:03.585
So, we dispose of the grease

00:03:03.585 --> 00:03:05.276
at a water-pollution-control facility

00:03:05.276 --> 00:03:07.320
that will accept the
fats, oil, and grease.

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:10.030
And there's not many
places that do accept it,

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:13.520
so it's limited, but we dispose of it

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:15.420
in an environmentally responsible way.

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:21.788
When grease goes down the drain

00:03:21.788 --> 00:03:23.580
with the dishwater, it's typically hot.

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:27.163
So, grease or oils, when
they're hot, they liquefy.

00:03:27.163 --> 00:03:29.845
When they cool down, they
solidify in the drain.

00:03:29.845 --> 00:03:31.040
That may cause plugging,

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:34.200
which will in turn cause
backups, overflows.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:36.290
If it negatively impacts the sewer system,

00:03:36.290 --> 00:03:38.320
then they can incur fines.

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:41.110
They will have to buy a new grease trap

00:03:41.110 --> 00:03:44.708
or pay plumbing fees to
rectify the problems.

